/**
\fn uint32 pdg::SpriteLayer::getSerializedSize(ISerializer* serializer) const
\brief Measure selected Layer state and shared Sprite resources.
\param serializer Writer used for resource policy and shared-object size tracking; not null.
\return Record size in bytes, excluding the stream header and enclosing object tag/length.
Uses the Layer's serialization flags. Initial records validate supported state
and resource availability during sizing.
\sa SpriteLayer::serialize(), SpriteLayer::setSerializationFlags()
*/

/**
\fn void pdg::SpriteLayer::serialize(ISerializer* serializer) const
\brief Save selected Layer state and its ordered sprites.
\param serializer Destination writer; not null. Select its resource mode before writing.
The default `ser_Full` includes Layer draw transforms, initial identities, frame
artwork, playback, programmed tweens, root and Part PhysicsBody state and active drives.
Remaining Layer fade completion delays use seconds. Shared images are written
once and use the writer's embedded-resource or external-reference policy.


Updates with `ser_Animations` (including `ser_Update`) transfer sampled local Part
transforms, reflection, constant movement and scheduled animations by Part ID.
They preserve existing Part identities, parent links and artwork. This includes
poses already solved by Part IK. The receiver can stop Layer animations and
disable collisions to display only the received state. Part creation/removal,
reparenting, artwork changes, IK controller configuration and Part body/constraint
state require another initial snapshot; incremental Part records are not full
physics checkpoints. A changed Part ID set is rejected.

Independent Parts retain IDs/names, parent links, transforms, tweens and shared
Image/Drawing content. Parent links resolve before body publication. Nested
Drawing geometry/attributes are embedded; referenced images use the save policy.
Rigid mounts retain their mounting Part, child Sprite, sampled child grip frame,
error/held pose and nested relationships. Children retain their independent layer
draw order. Mount links resolve after all Sprite/Part identities exist; nonphysical
and kinematic children retain the same ownership and command restrictions.

Drawing colors and image transparency colors use eight-bit channels, with opaque
alpha omitted. Floating-point colors can change by less than one channel step
(1/255) when restored. Image pixel data is preserved exactly.
Drawing geometry, pixel-space attributes (including thickness, text size, offsets
and transform translation), and image subsections use the compact coordinate
serializers. These can discard a fractional remainder smaller than 0.001 per
encoded coordinate component. Rectangles encode their origin and size, so those
small errors can accumulate at the opposite edge. Physics state, collision
geometry, constraint anchors, IK inputs and Sprite/Part transforms retain their
existing floating-point precision.

Packed boolean flags omit default values and absent payloads. Repeated images,
Drawing storage and imported animation assets use shared object references within
the same serializer stream. This preserves shared identity and avoids writing
the same resource again; independently created objects remain distinct.


Imported SCML animations retain their shared model, selected entity/clip,
floating-point playback time, pause/events, character maps, reference pose, bone
overrides, blends and interrupted transitions. Bone-, artwork- and socket-bound
Parts resolve against the restored rig. Built-in rig IK modifiers and retained
Drawing registrations preserve their IDs, options and enabled state. Animation
colliders continue following the restored pose.

Complete saves embed the imported document and its images and can load without
the source files. Embedded XML is compressed losslessly with zlib when that makes
it smaller; decompression is automatic. Documents are limited to 64 MiB before
compression, and corrupt payloads or mismatched lengths cause a load error.
External-reference saves omit the XML, resolve the original document path and
verify that its contents have not changed; images use the selected resource policy.
Missing or changed dependencies cause a load error.

Initial records preserve Collider shapes and shape IDs, frame/animation-source options,
material overrides, collision filters, sensor/enabled state and contact-event
opt-in. Compound-body associations, all PhysicsConstraint types, linked IK limits
and active physical Part IK controllers resolve after all owners exist. Captured
IK offsets and sampled drives survive; offsets are not recaptured from a deformed
pose. Geometry is stored in both resource modes; frame images follow the resource
policy. Restored colliders receive new process-wide IDs.

Callback collision filters/handlers are rejected; reinstall application handlers
after loading. Solver contact caches and pending contact events are not saved,
so loading rebuilds contacts and can produce new Begin events. A resumed simulation
is not guaranteed to be bit-for-bit identical to an uninterrupted one.

\note Initial saves support frame-based and imported Sprite collections with
Parts, colliders, constraints, physical Part IK and rigid mounts. Active imported
physical rigs, older attachSprite() associations, modifier/drawing callbacks,
custom easing/helpers, Font attributes, custom Drawing implementations and links
to other layers are rejected. Drawing content requires a GUI-capable build. Port assignment, event handlers and shared-world gravity and
damping configuration belong to the receiving application. Discard the writer
after a save error.

\code{.cpp}
// layer is an existing SpriteLayer without unsupported associations.
Serializer writer;
writer.setResourceMode(serialization_Complete);
layer->setSerializationFlags(ser_Full);
layer->serialize(&writer);
// Copy the bytes into storage owned by a Deserializer, then call
// restoredLayer->deserialize(&reader) on a layer created with createSpriteLayer().
\endcode
\sa SpriteLayer::deserialize(), ISerializer::setResourceMode()
*/

/**
\fn void pdg::SpriteLayer::deserialize(IDeserializer* deserializer)
\brief Restore Layer state or apply an incremental update.
\param deserializer Reader positioned at a SpriteLayer record; not null.
Initial records load into an empty or populated Layer, preserving shared resources, independent Part graphs
and Sprite order. Mount links are validated after creating the entire collection,
including cycle/duplicate-owner checks. Record/resource validation completes before replacing live
membership and state. Retained old sprites are detached; restored bodies join
the solver after validation. Layer tween pointers, including zoom, are rebound to
the receiving object. A validation failure preserves existing state; discard the
failed reader.

The destination keeps its Port, event handlers and caller-selected serialization
flags. Incremental updates require matching sprites and are applied directly.
\note Call outside animation modifiers and a physical solve. The shared physical
world is not recreated; configure gravity and damping in the application.
\sa SpriteLayer::serialize(), SpriteLayer::setSerializationFlags()
*/
